## This Year's Recall Landscape

The numbers don't lie – we're on track to see food recalls surpass 500 this year alone. That's nearly 60% of all historical recalls on record, crammed into just 12 months. To put it mildly, 2026 has been a banner year for food safety incidents.

## Salmonella and Allergens Lead the Pack

Breaking down the recalls, Salmonella continues to reign as the most common cause, contributing to over 150 incidents. Hot on its heels, allergen-related recalls account for 246 cases this year. Why the spike? Supply chain complexities, globally sourced ingredients, and less-than-perfect oversight are certainly suspects.

## Serial Offenders: When Lightning Strikes Twice... or More?

The list of repeat offenders reads like a who's-who of the food industry: Ghirardelli Chocolate Company and Albertsons Companies LLC both with 11 recalls in 2026 alone, closely followed by Moonlight Packing Corporation. These companies should know better, but it seems that the lessons are either too expensive to learn or simply ignored.

## Most Recent Recalls (as of May 27)

Some of the headline-grabbing recalls this week include:

- **Ghirardelli Chocolate Company**: A variety of powdered mixes (including hot cocoa and white chocolate powder) are at risk of Salmonella contamination. 
- **Wellnov Supplements LLC**: Liposomal sprays such as the Sleep Spray and B12 Spray are flagged for possible bacterial pathogen contamination.
- **Solina U.S. Holding**: Multiple seasoning products show potential for Salmonella contamination.

## Conclusion

With the food recall landscape growing increasingly fraught, it's vital to pay close attention to announcements from the FDA and food safety watchdogs. And to the repeat offenders on the list: do better. The cost of negligence is far too high.
